\paragraph{Relaxation, Divergence, and SPON.}
At 50\%, Gumbel-Sigmoid outperforms STE~\cite{bengio2013ste} by 33.8\% (11.79 vs.\ 15.77); forward KL outperforms JSD (+6.4\%) and reverse KL (+8.6\%), consistent with mode-covering~\cite{gu2024minillm}. % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42, jsd_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42
At 30\%, forward KL (8.81) and JSD (8.90) are near-identical, both outperforming reverse KL (9.26) and MSE (18.52$\pm$1.68). % exp_id: kl30_ablation_s42, jsd30_ablation_s42, rkl30_ablation_s42, mse30_uniform_s42, mse30_3seed
KL--JSD near-equivalence suggests the divergence measure matters less than optimizing through the output distribution; coupling scope may provide additional downstream benefits (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}). % exp_id: bce30_uniform_s42, rkl30_downstream_3seed
\paragraph{Per-Layer KL: Signal Type vs.\ Coupling Scope.}
\label{sec:perlayer}
To isolate cross-layer coupling, we train per-layer KL predictors that mask $K$ random layers per step and compute KL on the full model output, preserving the output-distribution signal without cross-layer gradients.
The $K$-curve (Figure~\ref{fig:k_curve_ppl}) reveals three empirical patterns at 30\%: (1)~PPL is non-monotonic, with $K{=}2$ worst (10.75$\pm$0.15) due to insufficient per-layer updates and $K{=}16$ best (8.67$\pm$0.02), marginally outperforming E2E (8.81$\pm$0.02); (2)~at $K{=}1$ (no coupling), per-layer KL achieves 9.05$\pm$0.02, within 2.7\% of E2E, while per-layer MSE degrades to 18.52$\pm$1.68 ($2.05\times$ worse); (3)~the signal type\footnote{We use ``signal type'' as shorthand for the training objective's optimization target (output distribution vs.\ intermediate activations); this dimension also entails differences in gradient flow topology and optimization landscape that are not independently controlled.} gap ($K{=}1$ KL 9.05 vs.\ MSE 18.52: 9.47 PPL) dwarfs the coupling breadth range ($K{=}16$ 8.67 vs.\ $K{=}2$ 10.75: 2.08 PPL).
This suggests output-distribution alignment contributes more than coupling scope to PPL, but the decomposition is confounded by train--inference mismatch ($K{=}1$ masks 1/32 layers during training vs.\ all 32 at inference), per-layer update count disparity, and the 50\% failure where $K{=}1$ (34.85) outperforms $K{=}4$ (44.23) while E2E reaches 11.84.
A 3-seed $K{=}4$ control\footnote{Actual sparsity 29.6\%; per-layer sparsity std=0.003 (near-uniform).} partially deconfounds (Appendix~\ref{app:k4_downstream}): PPL and GSM8K gaps largely close while commonsense gaps (ARC-c, WG) persist, suggesting task-dependent coupling effects; detailed per-task attribution is in Appendices~\ref{app:k4_downstream}--\ref{app:perlayer_downstream}. % exp_id: perlayer_kl_30_3seed, perlayer_kl_downstream, mse30_downstream_decomp, perlayer_kl_k4_3seed, perlayer_kl_k4_50_s42, perlayer_kl_50_s42, bench_kl50_global, k_curve_k2_3seed, k_curve_k8_3seed, k_curve_k16_3seed

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{k_curve_ppl}
\vspace{-8pt}
\caption{Coupling breadth $K$ vs.\ perplexity (30\%, TEAL uniform, 3-seed mean$\pm$std). $K{=}32$ denotes end-to-end training. $K{=}2$ degradation reflects insufficient per-layer updates (${\sim}62$ vs.\ ${\sim}250$ at $K{=}8$).}
\label{fig:k_curve_ppl}
\vspace{-8pt}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Compensation Redundancy.}
\label{sec:compensation}
FastForward-style compensation~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} (+67.2M params) provides no benefit (Appendices~\ref{app:comp_convergence}--\ref{app:training_convergence}). % exp_id: kl_comp_30_s42, kl_comp_30_s456, kl_comp_50_s42, bce_comp_staged_s42, db_sensitivity_sweep, tau_schedule_sweep, training_steps_sweep


\subsection{Sparsity Scaling and Regime Analysis}
\label{sec:scaling}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_sparsity_sweep.pdf}
\vspace{-8pt}
\caption{PPL scaling with sparsity (global allocation). KL's advantage over TEAL grows with sparsity.}
\label{fig:sparsity_sweep}
\vspace{-8pt}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Allocation Behavior.}
Table~\ref{tab:phase} (Appendix~\ref{app:alloc_full}) reveals non-monotonic allocation preference: uniform is best at $\leq$30\% ($7.3\times$ smaller MMLU $\sigma$), global at 35--40\%, both collapse at $\geq$45\%. % exp_id: kl30_3seed, regime_kl35_global, regime_kl35_uniform, multi_seed_kl50_global, multi_seed_kl50_uniform

\paragraph{Cross-Architecture and Scale.}
\label{sec:cross_arch}
On Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral}, PPL generalizes ($-$17.3\%, 3-seed; Table~\ref{tab:mistral_downstream}); on Qwen-2.5-14B~\cite{qwen2024qwen25}, PPL generalizes but MMLU/GSM8K decline (Table~\ref{tab:14b}).
Consistent regression across scales (8B MMLU $-$5.2pp, 14B GSM8K 32.2$\pm$5.3 vs.\ 52.1) suggests systematic limitations of output-distribution KL. The pipeline adds 6.4\% latency overhead absent sparse kernels (Appendix~\ref{app:latency}). % exp_id: mistral_kl30_s42, mistral_kl30_s123, mistral_kl30_s456, mistral_teal30_downstream, qwen14b_kl30_uniform_4task, qwen14b_teal30_uniform, latency_profiling

