\section{Taxonomy Discussion}
\label{app:taxonomy}

\paragraph{Correction granularity.}
\emph{Static} methods~\cite{xu2025spon} learn a fixed bias vector per layer that does not depend on the input token.
\emph{Per-token} methods~\cite{liu2023dejavu,gautam2026fastforward,akhauri2024shadowllm,szatkowski2024d2dmoe} produce a unique mask for each input position, conditioning on the token's hidden state at each layer.

\paragraph{Training signal coupling.}
\emph{Decomposed} objectives (BCE) treat each neuron independently, optimizing per-element classification against an oracle mask.
\emph{Intermediate} objectives (MSE on hidden states) capture local coupling within a single layer but not end-to-end output effects.
\emph{Coupled} objectives (KL on output distribution) propagate gradients through the full forward pass; each mask decision's gradient reflects its joint effect with all other masks on the final distribution.

SPON~\cite{xu2025spon} distills a per-layer bias via KL but cannot adapt to per-input neuron importance (static $\times$ coupled).
DejaVu~\cite{liu2023dejavu} and FastForward~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} achieve per-token granularity but use BCE, where each neuron's gradient is independent of other mask states.
D2DMoE~\cite{szatkowski2024d2dmoe} provides intermediate coupling (MSE on hidden states) but not end-to-end output effects.
We define \emph{output-distribution alignment} as whether the training loss optimizes a quantity that composes across layers.
KL on output logits directly optimizes next-token prediction loss, which does not suffer from error composition; per-layer MSE minimizes reconstruction error independently at each layer, but layer-wise optima do not compose---a mask minimizing FFN output error at layer $\ell$ may amplify errors in layers $\ell{+}1 \ldots L$.
The SNR analysis (\S\ref{sec:method:gradient}) corroborates this: KL achieves the best masks despite $2.3\times$ lower SNR than BCE, while MSE produces the worst masks.
The framework predicts that losses operating on output-level quantities should outperform intermediate-level losses, a hypothesis consistent with our MSE results and testable with alternative output-level objectives.

\section{Oracle Mask Provenance}
\label{app:oracle_provenance}

C1's oracle masks are extracted from a converged KL predictor and binarized via hard threshold (logits $> 0$, equivalent to $\sigma > 0.5$); they reflect one specific training trajectory (optimizer state, data order, Gumbel noise).
The s42/s123/s456 consistency ($\leq$1.70pp) provides partial evidence of robustness, but does not rule out trajectory-dependent confounds.

\section{SPON Orthogonality}
\label{app:spon}

SPON~\cite{xu2025spon} bias correction is weakly correlated with the KL predictor (cosine similarity 0.196, $R^2$=0.038) and yields only 2.4\% PPL improvement (12.08$\to$11.79); the predictor already captures most recoverable signal. % exp_id: spon_decomposition, spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42
The decomposition confirms that SPON and the KL predictor operate on largely orthogonal error sources.

\section{Per-Layer KL Downstream Decomposition}
\label{app:perlayer_downstream}

Table~\ref{tab:perlayer_downstream} reports raw downstream numbers for the signal type vs.\ coupling scope decomposition at 30\% sparsity (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}). BoolQ's $\approx$100\% coupling is an upper bound confounded by high cross-seed variance ($\pm$11.5pp for per-layer KL); the $K{=}4$ pilot (Table~\ref{tab:k4_downstream}) suggests much of this gap is train--inference mismatch.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{2D decomposition at 30\% sparsity (LLaMA-3.1-8B, uniform allocation). All rows are 3-seed mean$\pm$std. Signal type = per-layer KL $-$ MSE; coupling = E2E KL $-$ per-layer KL. Percentages are upper bounds (dimensions not fully orthogonal; see \S\ref{sec:perlayer}).}
\label{tab:perlayer_downstream}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
& PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K & BoolQ \\
\midrule
E2E KL & 8.81$\pm$0.02 & 43.7 & 68.9 & 40.7 & 7.5 & 73.7 \\
Per-layer KL & 9.05$\pm$0.02 & 39.9 & 65.9 & 38.6 & 5.8 & 64.5 \\
MSE & 18.52$\pm$1.68 & 35.8$\pm$4.1 & 59.8$\pm$2.6 & 30.7$\pm$2.6 & 2.3$\pm$0.7 & 64.5$\pm$5.3 \\
\midrule
Total gap & 9.71 & 7.9 & 9.1 & 10.0 & 5.2 & 9.2 \\
Signal type (\%) & 97.5 & 51.9 & 67.0 & 79.0 & 67.3 & $\approx$0 \\
Coupling (\%) & 2.5 & 48.1 & 33.0 & 21.0 & 32.7 & $\approx$100 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\section{$K{=}4$ Mismatch Control}
\label{app:k4_downstream}

Table~\ref{tab:k4_downstream} reports 3-seed $K{=}4$ per-layer KL results at 30\% sparsity.
Training with $K{=}4$ random layers per step reduces the train--inference mismatch (4/32 vs.\ 1/32 layers masked during training, while inference applies all 32).
The gap closure column reports the fraction of the $K{=}1 \to$ E2E KL gap closed by $K{=}4$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{3-seed $K{=}4$ mismatch control at 30\% sparsity (LLaMA-3.1-8B, uniform allocation, $s \in \{42, 123, 456\}$). Actual sparsity 29.6\%; per-layer sparsity std=0.003 (near-uniform). Gap closure $= (K{=}4 - K{=}1) / (\text{E2E} - K{=}1) \times 100$.}
\label{tab:k4_downstream}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
& $K{=}4$ (3-seed) & $K{=}1$ (3-seed) & E2E KL (3-seed) & Gap closure (\%) \\
\midrule
PPL$\downarrow$ & 8.89$\pm$0.01 & 9.05$\pm$0.02 & 8.81$\pm$0.02 & 66.7 \\
ARC-c & 40.0$\pm$1.3 & 39.9 & 43.7$\pm$2.5 & 2.6 \\
WG & 66.9$\pm$0.2 & 65.9 & 68.9$\pm$0.4 & 33.3 \\
MMLU$^\ast$ & 42.7$\pm$1.4 & 38.6 & 40.7$\pm$1.3 & $>$100 \\
GSM8K & 7.0$\pm$1.6 & 5.8 & 7.5$\pm$0.1 & 70.6 \\
BoolQ$^\dagger$ & 67.7$\pm$7.5 & 64.5$\pm$11.5 & 73.7 & 34.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize $^\ast$MMLU $K{=}4$ systematically exceeds E2E across all 3 seeds; attributable to $K{=}4$'s near-uniform per-layer sparsity (std=0.003) matching uniform eval allocation. Independently confirmed: removing the predictor's middle-layer valley yields MMLU=44.0\% (Appendix~\ref{app:constraint}).
$^\dagger$BoolQ exhibits high inter-seed variance for both $K{=}4$ (std=7.5) and $K{=}1$ (std=11.5); gap closure unreliable.}
\end{table}

\section{Per-Layer Sparsity Constraint Analysis}
\label{app:constraint}

To test the causal role of the KL predictor's learned per-layer allocation, we evaluate two inference-time interventions at 30\% overall sparsity (seed 42, uniform allocation baseline):

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-layer sparsity constraint analysis. \emph{Protect late}: cap L22--L28 sparsity $\leq$30\%; \emph{Remove valley}: override L14--L18 sparsity to 30\%. Budget redistributed to maintain 30\% overall.}
\label{tab:constraint}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Condition & PPL & ARC-c & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
KL baseline (s42) & 8.82 & 40.8 & 40.6 & 7.4 \\
Protect late layers & 9.26 & 42.4 & 40.7 & 6.5 \\
Remove valley & 8.76 & 39.3 & 44.0 & 9.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Protecting late layers (L22--L28) does not improve knowledge-intensive metrics despite reducing their sparsity from 48--57\% to $\leq$30\%, while worsening PPL by 5.2\%.
Conversely, removing the protection valley (forcing L14--L18 to 30\% instead of 14--18\%) improves MMLU by +3.4pp and GSM8K by +1.6pp at the cost of ARC-c $-$1.5pp.
The KL predictor's learned allocation thus represents a task-dependent trade-off---prioritizing commonsense reasoning via middle-layer protection---rather than a uniformly optimal pattern.
This allocation trade-off also explains the $K{=}4$ MMLU anomaly (\S\ref{app:k4_downstream}): $K{=}4$'s near-uniform learned sparsity (std=0.003) inherently removes the valley, producing MMLU=43.5 comparable to the remove-valley condition (44.0).

\section{Per-Layer Sparsity Distribution}
\label{app:perlayer_sparsity}

The KL predictor at 30\% target sparsity learns a non-uniform per-layer allocation that differs markedly from magnitude-based selection.
Aggregate sparsity is similar across broad regions (early L0--L7: KL 28.1\% vs.\ magnitude 27.5\%; late L24--L31: 44.9\% vs.\ 47.2\%), but middle layers (L8--L23) exhibit high internal variance despite similar averages (28.2\% vs.\ 27.3\%).
Table~\ref{tab:perlayer_sparsity} highlights three sub-ranges.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-layer sparsity sub-ranges: KL predictor vs.\ magnitude (LLaMA-3.1-8B, 30\%, uniform allocation). Ranges denote min--max across layers in each group.}
\label{tab:perlayer_sparsity}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Layer Range & KL (\%) & Mag (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
L8--L12 (high pruning) & 32--36 & 25--26 & $+$7 to $+$10 \\
L14--L18 (protection valley) & 14--18 & 28--30 & $-$8 to $-$16 \\
L22--L28 (aggressive pruning) & 48--57 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{C4 Training Data Control}
\label{app:c4_control}

To test whether WikiText-103's narrow domain drives the knowledge-task regression (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), we train the KL predictor on C4~\cite{raffel2020c4} (diverse web text) under identical hyperparameters (1000 steps, 30\% uniform, s42).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{C4-trained vs.\ WikiText-trained KL predictor (LLaMA-3.1-8B, 30\% uniform, s42).}
\label{tab:c4_control}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Training Data & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K & HS / PIQA \\
\midrule
WikiText-103 & 8.82 & 40.78 & 68.59 & 40.6 & 7.35 & 70.85 / 74.81 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_uniform_hellaswag_piqa
C4 & 11.55 & 43.09 & 68.67 & 39.95 & 4.09 & 69.24 / 75.03 \\ % exp_id: c4_kl30_s42_eval
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The C4-trained predictor achieves comparable MMLU (40.0\% vs.\ 40.6\%) but worse GSM8K (4.1\% vs.\ 7.4\%) and higher PPL (11.55 vs.\ 8.82), despite training on more diverse text.
This rules out training data composition as the primary cause of knowledge-task regression and supports the hypothesis that forward KL's mode-covering property structurally under-protects rare reasoning-critical activations. % exp_id: c4_control_eval

\section{Per-Layer Coupling Breadth Analysis}
\label{app:k_curve}

We vary the number of layers coupled per training step ($K$) from 1 (pure per-layer) to 32 (end-to-end), evaluating all predictors under TEAL uniform allocation at 30\% sparsity on LLaMA-3.1-8B.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{K-curve: coupling breadth vs.\ predictor quality (30\%, TEAL uniform, LLaMA-3.1-8B). All values are 3-seed mean$\pm$std (s42/s123/s456) except $K{=}1$ downstream (single seed). ``---'' = not evaluated.}
\label{tab:k_curve}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{cccccccc}
\toprule
$K$ & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K & HS & PIQA \\
\midrule
1 & 9.05$\pm$0.02 & 39.9 & 65.9 & 38.6 & 5.8 & --- & --- \\
2 & 10.75$\pm$0.15 & 39.7$\pm$1.2 & 63.2$\pm$1.7 & 38.3$\pm$3.2 & 2.8$\pm$0.6 & 66.5$\pm$1.0 & 73.5$\pm$0.7 \\
4 & 8.89$\pm$0.01 & 40.0$\pm$1.2 & 66.9$\pm$0.2 & 42.7$\pm$1.4 & 7.0$\pm$1.6 & 67.8$\pm$1.0 & 74.5$\pm$0.5 \\
8 & 9.00$\pm$0.06 & 40.7$\pm$1.0 & 68.7$\pm$1.3 & 40.4$\pm$0.6 & 6.8$\pm$1.3 & 69.0$\pm$1.1 & 75.1$\pm$1.1 \\
16 & 8.67$\pm$0.02 & 41.9$\pm$1.1 & 68.5$\pm$0.9 & 40.9$\pm$2.9 & 8.5$\pm$1.2 & 70.1$\pm$0.4 & 75.0$\pm$0.8 \\
32 (E2E) & 8.81$\pm$0.02 & 43.7$\pm$2.5 & 68.9$\pm$0.4 & 40.7$\pm$1.3 & 7.5$\pm$0.1 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

PPL exhibits a non-monotonic U-shape (Figure~\ref{fig:k_curve_ppl}): $K{=}2$ is worst (10.75$\pm$0.15), likely due to insufficient per-layer updates (${\sim}$62 updates/layer vs.\ ${\sim}$250 at $K{=}8$). $K{=}16$ achieves the lowest PPL (8.67$\pm$0.02), marginally better than E2E (8.81$\pm$0.02). $K{=}16$ MMLU exhibits high seed variance (std=2.9; s42=44.0, s123=40.0, s456=38.7), with the 3-seed mean (40.9) indistinguishable from E2E (40.7$\pm$1.3).
The signal type gap ($K{=}1$ KL 9.05 vs.\ per-layer MSE 18.52$\pm$1.68) of 9.47 PPL dwarfs the coupling breadth range within KL (best $K{=}16$ 8.67 vs.\ worst $K{=}2$ 10.75 = 2.08 PPL), reinforcing that output-distribution alignment, not coupling scope, is the primary quality lever for PPL at this sparsity level. % exp_id: k_curve_k2_3seed, k_curve_k8_3seed, k_curve_k16_3seed, perlayer_kl_30_3seed, perlayer_kl_k4_3seed

